e the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\hat{\\sigma} = \\sqrt{2.25} = 1.5.\n$$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443479 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443485 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443486 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443488 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443480 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443481 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443482 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443483 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443484 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184208 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443478 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184208 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443487 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184405 1050 540.0 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184870 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n4,\\,8,\\,10,\\,6,\\,7\n$$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186184870 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$4, 8, 10, 6, 7$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534902 17592186045417 false] [17592186184870 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(4+8+10+6+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{265}{5} \\\\  & =53.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =53-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $5.$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186185326 1064 "Recall that for a sample of size $n$, the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2\n$$\n\nWe will now prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$, that is,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[S^2\\right] = \\sigma^2\n$$\n\nA major step towards this goal is to find the expected value of the sum of squares term:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E \\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 \\right] \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFirst, consider the following sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this term by adding and subtracting the sample mean $\\overline X$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\left((X_i - \\overline X) + (\\overline X - \\mu)\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSquaring and applying the laws of summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)((X_{i} - X^(―)) + (X^(―) - μ))^{2} &=  \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)((X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + 2(X_{i} - X^(―))(X^(―) - μ) + (X^(―) - μ)^{2}) &=  \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + 2∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))(X^(―) - μ) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X^(―) - μ)^{2} &=  \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + 2(X^(―) - μ)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―)) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X^(―) - μ)^{2} &=  \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X^(―) - μ)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + n(X^(―) - μ)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i - \\overline X) = 0\n$$\n\nand that $(\\overline X - \\mu)$ does not depend on $i$. Thus, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 + n(\\overline X- \\mu)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 - n(\\overline X- \\mu)^2. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast\\ast)\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side is the summation term $(\\ast)$ we wish to find the expectation of.\n\nNow, we make use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[(X_{i} - μ)^{2}] &= σ^{2} \\\\\nE[(X^(―) - μ)^{2}] &= Var[X^(―)] = \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinding the expectation of $(\\ast\\ast)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2}] &= E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - μ)^{2}] - nE[(X^(―) - μ)^{2}] \\\\\n&= nσ^{2} - n \\cdot \\frac{σ^{2}}{n} \\\\\n&= nσ^{2} - σ^{2} \\\\\n&= (n - 1)σ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nE[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2}] = ((n - 1)σ^{2})/(n)\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the statistic\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2\n$$\n\nis a *biased* estimate of $\\sigma^2$. Therefore, to form an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$, we must divide our summation term by $n-1$, not $n$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[S^{2}] &= E[\\frac{1}{n - 1}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1} \\cdot (n - 1)σ^{2} \\\\\n&= σ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139534902 17592186045417 false] [17592186185326 1064 "Recall that for a sample of size $n,$ the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2.\n$$\n\nWe will now prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2,$ that is,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[S^2\\right] = \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nA major step towards this goal is to find the expected value of the sum of squares term:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E \\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 \\right] \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFirst, consider the following sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this term by adding and subtracting the sample mean $\\overline X,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\left((X_i - \\overline X) + (\\overline X -  \\mu)\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSquaring and applying the laws of summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{(({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})+(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu ))}^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )+(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}) & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+n(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have used the fact that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i - \\overline X) = 0$ and that $(\\overline X - \\mu)$ does not depend on $i$. Thus, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 =  \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 + n(\\overline X-  \\mu)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 -  n(\\overline X-  \\mu)^2  . \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast\\ast)\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side is the summation term $(\\ast)$ we wish to find the expectation of.\n\nNow, we make use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}] & ={\\sigma }^{2} \\\\ \\text{E}[(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}] & =\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinding the expectation of $(\\ast\\ast),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}] & =\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}]-n\\text{E}[(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}] \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}-n\\cdot \\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n} \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}-{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =(n-1){\\sigma }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}]=\\frac{(n-1){\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the statistic\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2\n$$\n\nis a *biased* estimate of $\\sigma^2.$ Therefore, to form an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2,$ we must divide our summation term by $n-1,$ not $n.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{S}^{2}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}\\cdot (n-1){\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186185357 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n\nWe've already seen that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$, given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator of $\\mu$, which means that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\nThe definition of the sample mean follows naturally from the definition of $\\mu$.\n\nHowever, when constructing estimators for the population variance $\\sigma^2$, things are not so simple!\n\nFrom the definition of the population variance, you might expect that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator for $\\sigma^2$. However, it can be shown that it is a *biased* estimator of $\\sigma^2$, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\\right] \\neq \\sigma^2\n$$\n\nThis might seem surprising, but it's true.\n\nTo compute an *unbiased* estimate of $\\sigma^2$, we use the so-called **sample variance**. The sample variance is denoted $S^2$, and is defined as\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nAt the end of this lesson, we will prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Notice that instead of dividing by $n$ (as with our original biased estimator), we instead divide by $n-1$. This is sometimes referred to as **Bessel's correction**, as it corrects the bias of the original estimator.\n- We should think of $S^2$ as a random variable whose value varies according to the particular sample under consideration.\n- Since $S^2$ is a random variable, it has a sampling distribution (i.e., a probability distribution). We'll learn more about the sampling distribution of $S^2$ in future lessons.\n\nFinally, we have an alternative formula for the sample variance that's often more convenient in practice:\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left(\\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson." 13194139534902 17592186045417 false] [17592186185357 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ \n\nWe've already seen that the sample mean $\\overline{X},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator of $\\mu,$ which means that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\nThe definition of the sample mean follows naturally from the definition of $\\mu.$\n\nHowever, when constructing estimators for the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ things are not so simple! \n\nFrom the definition of the population variance, you might expect that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator for $\\sigma^2.$ However, it can be shown that it is a *biased* estimator of $\\sigma^2,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\\right] \\neq \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nThis might seem surprising, but it's true. \n\nTo compute an *unbiased* estimate of $\\sigma^2,$ we use the so-called **sample variance**. The sample variance is denoted $S^2,$ and is defined as\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nAt the end of this lesson, we will prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Notice that instead of dividing by $n$ (as with our original biased estimator), we instead divide by $n-1.$ This is sometimes referred to as **Bessel's correction**, as it corrects the bias of the original estimator.\n\n- We should think of $S^2$ as a random variable whose value varies according to the particular sample under consideration.\n\n- Since $S^2$ is a random variable, it has a sampling distribution (i.e., a probability distribution). We'll learn more about the sampling distribution of $S^2$ in future lessons.\n\nFinally, we have an alternative formula for the sample variance that's often more convenient in practice:\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson." 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186185358 1064 "Here, we will show that\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right].\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of $S^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses and distributing the summation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}^{2}-2{X}_{i}\\overset{―}{X}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2{X}_{i}\\overset{―}{X}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}-2\\overset{―}{X}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now consider each sum separately:\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2 = n\\cdot \\overline{X^2}$ follows directly from the  definition of the mean of $X^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i = n\\cdot \\overline{X}$ follows directly from the  definition of the mean of $X$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\, \\textrm{times}} = n$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}-2\\overset{―}{X}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[n\\cdot \\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-2\\overset{―}{X}\\cdot n\\cdot \\overset{―}{X}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}\\cdot n] \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-2{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186185358 1064 "Here, we will show that\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left(\\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of $S^2$:\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses and distributing the summation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{2} &= \\frac{1}{n - 1}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{2} - 2X_{i}X^(―) + (X^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{2} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)2X_{i}X^(―) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X^(―))^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{2} - 2X^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i} + (X^(―))^{2} \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)1]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now consider each sum separately:\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2 = n\\cdot \\overline{X^2}$ follows directly from the definition of the mean of $X^2$\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i = n\\cdot \\overline{X}$ follows directly from the definition of the mean of $X$\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\, \\textrm{times}} = n$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{2} &= \\frac{1}{n - 1}[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{2} - 2X^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i} + (X^(―))^{2} \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)1] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}[n \\cdot X^{2}^(―) - 2X^(―) \\cdot n \\cdot X^(―) + (X^(―))^{2} \\cdot n] \\\\\n&= \\frac{n}{n - 1}[X^{2}^(―) - 2(X^(―))^{2} + (X^(―))^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{n}{n - 1}[X^{2}^(―) - (X^(―))^{2}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139534902 17592186045417 false] [17592186443412 1080 "16" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443412 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443412 1083 #uuid "070b0ec1-e144-5414-93ef-c5d5462a2e0d" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443413 1080 "2.75" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443413 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443413 1083 #uuid "138278bc-8f58-5839-98b8-c61a9d2415ad" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443414 1080 "12" 131941395