n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(19-14+22-21) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4}({19}^{2}+(-14)^{2}+{22}^{2}+(-21)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,482}{4} \\\\  & =370.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =370.5-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =368.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4-1}\\cdot 368.25 \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,473}{3} \\\\  & =491.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $491.$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443471 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443486 1069 "q-89001" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443486 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n-6,\\,7,\\,10,\\,-9,\\,8\n$$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443486 1072 #uuid "9d145845-d1fd-557d-b5dc-7262bedf9b90" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443486 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(-6+7+10-9+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}[(-6)^{2}+{7}^{2}+{10}^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{8}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{330}{5} \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =66-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =62.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 62 \\\\  & =\\frac{310}{4} \\\\  & =77.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $77.5.$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443470 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443487 1069 "q-139847" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443487 1071 "A random sample of size $n=11$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{11}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=220\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere each ${x}_{i}$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance." 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443487 1072 #uuid "c0d91061-b2d9-57bd-a897-ea0b99d019a6" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443487 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{11}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=220$ and $n=11.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11-1}\\cdot 220 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 220 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $22.$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443472 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443488 1069 "q-88999" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443488 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n13,\\,15,\\,9,\\,11,\\,12\n$$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443488 1072 #uuid "c16ee770-6755-511d-a2bf-8ccbcb77f364" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443488 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(13+15+9+11+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{5} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({13}^{2}+{15}^{2}+{9}^{2}+{11}^{2}+{12}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{740}{5} \\\\  & =148.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =148-{12}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $5.$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-070b0ec1-e144-5414-93ef-c5d5462a2e0d" 17592186443412 "answer-138278bc-8f58-5839-98b8-c61a9d2415ad" 17592186443413 "answer-15c9d4c7-4932-51cb-851e-20bc41eff0b1" 17592186443414 "answer-15dde39d-88be-58d2-9cfe-766d0c016346" 17592186443415 "answer-1d937d0f-642b-5b54-95f1-386570968e23" 17592186443416 "answer-2434cf21-1670-5f80-a111-bc450c348b45" 17592186443417 "answer-26f31e5b-1fa1-56d2-8786-d2c285ab5fa8" 17592186443418 "answer-28bebf10-4736-5d5b-991d-68bf2ddce06d" 17592186443419 "answer-3009f909-e476-50ba-a047-197b6a85673b" 17592186443420 "answer-4782e79d-2978-56f4-8596-d71632fec800" 17592186443421 "answer-4905b420-a0a9-5677-91f8-5b85481d8ce3" 17592186443422 "answer-4a233216-fa1d-56d4-9e2b-16a571302e53" 17592186443423 "answer-4df8442f-f169-58a3-b714-41e6cbd06201" 17592186443424 "answer-51fdafc4-a887-576d-9d4